d2d1: Build path geometry outlines when the geometry is first stroked.

EndFigure() built each figure's stroke outline at once, whether or not
the geometry would ever be stroked. Affinity rebuilds thousands of filled
paths a second (glyph and ruler outlines), and while painting a brush
stroke Direct2D grew its arrays about 650,000 times a second, moving
300 MB/s; the outline arrays were nearly half of that, plus the work of
the outline itself.

Build the outline when something first draws it instead: DrawGeometry(),
directly or through a transformed geometry or a geometry group, which
now take their source's outline. Close() reworks the figures for the fill
(self-intersections, overlapping beziers), so it first keeps them as they
were ended: on a close-cache hit by taking the arrays the cached result
replaces, otherwise as copies. If they cannot be kept the outline is
built at once, as before. A lock serialises the build between threads.

The d2d1 conformance tests give the same result before and after (17491
tests, 0 failures). Affinity's interface, selection outlines and handles
render identically. Brush tip behind the cursor, nested KWin, Arc A750,
OpenCL on, 18 strokes each: median 39 -> 28 ms; UI thread CPU per stroke
down 8%.

